  "raw": " And if you agree with most of its big picture they’ve put forward, and think Roger Waters is the real leader of Pink Floyd, you’ll really enjoy Doomed Flamingoes.\n\nRoger Waters is one of the all-time greats, aside from being one of the all-time best songwriters in rock and roll. But the world he describes (and writes about) in this book might be the first real examination of the disastrous aborted and ultimately consummated second Pink Floyd album, The Division Bell. Waters is especially scathing in this book about David Gilmour’s musical career, praising Gilmour as a great guitar player and the actual The Division Bell album as a disappointment. But Waters himself admitted he didn’t know what to do with the studio that had housed the all instrumental album, aside from creating a piece of ambience, in a very clever and innovative way. Waters also says that while The Division Bell wasn’t",
  "serialized": "And if you agree with most of its big picture they’ve put forward, and think Roger Waters is the real leader of Pink Floyd, you’ll really enjoy Doomed Flamingoes. Roger Waters is one of the all-time greats, aside from being one of the all-time best songwriters in rock and roll. But the world he describes (and writes about) in this book might be the first real examination of the disastrous aborted and ultimately consummated second Pink Floyd album, The Division Bell. Waters is especially scathing in this book about David Gilmour’s musical career, praising Gilmour as a great guitar player and the actual The Division Bell album as a disappointment. But Waters himself admitted he didn’t know what to do with the studio that had housed the all instrumental album, aside from creating a piece of ambience, in a very clever and innovative way. Waters also says that while The Division Bell wasn’t",
